{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a2025"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c169): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c169.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a2025"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge science/maths","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/science__maths.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",1073],["+","\n\nThe circle also earns its keep far from compass and ruler. When the English logician John Venn published his diagrams for sets and logical statements in 1880, he chose one shape for them: the circle (overlapping circles), one for each set, with the overlap showing what the sets share. A Venn diagram of mammals and swimmers puts whales in the lens where the two circles cross, and the shape has become the universal picture of \"what these things have in common\"."]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",985],["+","\n\nAuch fern von Zirkel und Lineal leistet der Kreis gute Dienste. Als der englische Logiker John Venn 1880 seine Diagramme für Mengen und logische Aussagen veröffentlichte, wählte er dafür eine einzige Figur: den Kreis (sich überschneidende Kreise), einen für jede Menge, wobei die Überschneidung zeigt, was die Mengen gemeinsam haben. Ein Venn-Diagramm aus Säugetieren und Schwimmern setzt die Wale in die Linse, in der sich die beiden Kreise kreuzen, und die Figur ist zum allgemeinen Bild für „was diese Dinge gemeinsam haben“ geworden."]]}]},
